461	Watson Brake	-3000-01-01	\N	Watson Brake is a fascinating archaeological site located in Ouachita Parish, Louisiana. Here's a detailed breakdown of its significance: Age and Construction: Dating back to around 3500 BCE (over 5,400 years old!), Watson Brake is considered the oldest documented earthwork mound complex in North America. This predates iconic structures like the Egyptian pyramids and Stonehenge. The complex was constructed by the Evans culture, a group of hunter-gatherers from the Middle Archaic period. The sheer scale and complexity of the site challenge traditional views of these early societies. Mound Structure and Layout: Watson Brake features eleven oval and circular earthen mounds ranging from 3 to 25 feet in height. These mounds are arranged in a unique pattern, with some connected by ridges. Two curved ridges link two separate groups of mounds, forming a large enclosure measuring roughly 984 by 656 feet. The purpose and symbolism behind this layout are still being explored by archaeologists. Importance and Discoveries: The discovery and dating of Watson Brake in 1997 revolutionized understanding of early cultures in the Southeastern United States. It demonstrated that these societies possessed sophisticated social organization and the ability to undertake large-scale projects. Archaeological excavations at the site have uncovered various artifacts, including stone tools, beads, and animal remains. These offer valuable insights into the daily lives and practices of the Evans culture. Unanswered Questions and Ongoing Research: The exact function of the Watson Brake mounds remains a mystery. Theories suggest they could have served as ceremonial centers, gathering places, or even astronomical observatories. Ongoing research at the site continues to unearth new information about the Evans culture and their impressive construction techniques.	Ancient Americas	f	2025-06-23 16:09:10.169	2025-06-23 16:09:10.169
