527	Helmand and Jiroft culture	-2350-01-01	\N	The Helmand and Jiroft cultures were fascinating Bronze Age civilizations that flourished in the 3rd millennium BC (roughly 3300-2350 BC for Helmand and 3000-2200 BC for Jiroft). While geographically distinct, they share intriguing connections, potentially representing aspects of a broader cultural zone. Helmand Culture (Southern Afghanistan and Eastern Iran): Location: Flourished mainly in the middle and lower valley of the Helmand River, encompassing parts of modern-day southern Afghanistan (Kandahar, Helmand, and Nimruz provinces) and eastern Iran (Sistan and Baluchestan Province). Cities and Social Structure: Evidence suggests urban centers like Shahr-i Sokhta (Iran) and Mundigak (Afghanistan) with temples and palaces, hinting at a complex social structure. Material Culture: Known for vibrantly painted pottery featuring geometric patterns, plants, and animals. Bronze was also utilized, indicating technological advancement. Possible Connections: The Elamite texts found at Shahr-i Sokhta suggest connections with western Iran. Some scholars also propose links to the Indus Valley Civilization, although the timelines suggest the Helmand culture predates the Indus Valley's peak. Jiroft Culture (Eastern Iran): Location: Primarily flourished in the territory of present-day Sistan and Baluchestan and Kerman Provinces of Iran, with Konar Sandal near Jiroft being the proposed type site. Artistic Legacy: Renowned for its unique, highly detailed pottery featuring intricate designs including animals, human figures, and geometric patterns. Sculptures and impressive carved chlorite seals have also been unearthed. Intriguing Questions: The origin and sudden decline of the Jiroft culture remain topics of debate. Possible Link to Helmand: The cultural similarities between Jiroft and Helmand are striking, with some scholars proposing they might represent a single cultural sphere.	Ancient Near East	f	2025-06-23 16:09:10.536	2025-06-23 16:09:10.536
