576	City of Mari	-1811-01-01	\N	Founding a Trade Hub: Archaeologists believe Mari's founding stemmed from its strategic location on the west bank of the Euphrates River in Northern Mesopotamia (present-day eastern Syria) during the Early Bronze Age (around 3500-3000 BCE) and the Middle Bronze Age (around 3000-1550 BCE). This placed it perfectly for trade between Babylonia in southern Mesopotamia and the resource-rich Taurus Mountains of modern Turkey World History Encyclopedia. It's believed to have been one of the earliest planned cities, likely established as a trade hub and a center for copper and bronze-smelting World History Encyclopedia. Early Political Landscape (limited information): While details about the city's very beginnings are scarce, we do know Mari rose to prominence as a political entity by the mid-3rd millennium BCE Arch├⌐ologie | culture - Mari. Evidence from royal inscriptions in Sumerian cities, archives of Ebla (another prominent city-state), and documents unearthed in Mari itself point to a line of kings ruling Mari up until the last king of City II: Ishgi-Mari Arch├⌐ologie | culture - Mari. This early political history also suggests Mari was a rival of the powerful city of Ebla in western Syria between 2400 and 2300 BCE Arch├⌐ologie | culture - Mari. Destruction and Rebuilding: Around 2300 BCE, the Akkadian Empire, led by Sargon of Akkad, expanded and conquered Mari, destroying the city World History Encyclopedia. However, the Akkadians later allowed Mari to be rebuilt and even appointed a military governor to oversee it Wikipedia. With the Akkadian Empire's decline, Mari's governors became independent and rebuilt the city as a significant regional center along the Euphrates Wikipedia. Exploring Further: World History Encyclopedia: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mari,_Syria Arch├⌐ologie | culture - Mari: https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/mari/en Wikipedia: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mari,_Syria Britannica: https://www.britannica.com/place/Mari-ancient-city-Syria (This Britannica entry also mentions the excavations that unearthed much of our knowledge about Mari.)	General	f	2025-06-23 16:09:10.71	2025-06-23 16:09:10.71
